#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Team: parallel DAG orchestration across the swarm.

Where swarm.py answers "which single model should handle this prompt", team.py
answers "how do I get this whole body of work done in the least wall-clock
time, using every node at once".

The scheduling problem is: a task DAG, heterogeneous workers with different
measured speeds, and a reload penalty when a node has to swap which model it
holds. That is the classic heterogeneous DAG scheduling setup, and this uses a
HEFT-style list scheduler (Heterogeneous Earliest-Finish-Time): rank tasks by
their longest path to completion so the critical path starts first, then place
each task wherever it will actually finish soonest.

The practical consequence is the thing you want: the slow specialist starts on
the hard core immediately, and every peripheral chunk it doesn't need gets
packed onto other nodes inside that same window, so nothing is still being
computed when it's time to recombine.

    ./team.py split "summarise this file" src/*.py --reduce
    ./team.py run plan.json --dry-run
    ./team.py run plan.json
    ./team.py review "is this migration plan sound?  ..."

Standard library only, Python 3.9+. Requires swarm.py alongside it.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import ast
